(subtract one side, add another). So total operations ~ 2*S*(N-S+1)^2. That's O(S N^2) for large N.\n\nPrefix sum approach: Compute prefix sums P where P[i][j] = sum of matrix[0..i-1][0..j-1]. Then submatrix sum from (r,c) to (r+S-1, c+S-1) = P[r+S][c+S] - P[r][c+S] - P[r+S][c] + P[r][c]. That's O(1). So total time O(N^2) for prefix sums plus O((N-S+1)^2) for checking all submatrices. For N up to 8000, (N-S+1)^2 could be ~64e6 if S small, which is manageable. But baseline might already be fast enough? However, the problem statement says \"The proposed baseline algorithm employs a sliding window technique to reduce this complexity to O(SN^2).\" So they expect that approach. Using prefix sums would be an algorithmic change that could be faster but may not be considered legal if it changes the fundamental method? The rules say you can modify in any way, so it's allowed.\n\nBut our goal is SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies, not algorithm replacement. However, we could combine: use prefix sums to compute each submatrix sum with O(1) arithmetic, which might be vectorizable if we process multiple top-left corners simultaneously using SIMD? That would require restructuring loops.\n\nGiven the constraints of this experiment (we must propose one candidate), I think flattening and adding alignment hints is a safe transformation that may improve auto-vectorization. Also, we can add restrict qualifiers to pointers if we use raw arrays, but vector<vector> doesn't allow that easily.\n\nI'll also consider using OpenMP SIMD with reduction on the inner loops, but we already did that and got similar performance. Maybe combine flattening + OpenMP SIMD? But previous experiment used OpenMP pragmas without flattening and was correct and same speed as baseline. So maybe no improvement.\n\nAnother idea: Use loop interchange or unrolling to help vectorization. For row update, the inner loop over k is small (s up to N-1). We could manually unroll with a fixed factor? Not portable.\n\nGiven that we have limited time and need to propose something plausible, I'll go with flattening matrix into 1D array of ints allocated via std::vector<int> data(n*n); then read input directly into it. Then modify all accesses accordingly. This should improve cache locality for row updates and potentially allow auto-vectorization.\n\nAlso, we can add __builtin_assume_aligned on the pointer to indicate alignment (e.g., 16-byte aligned). But std::vector may not guarantee that; we could use posix_memalign or alignas. However, to keep it simple, we can just rely on vector's allocation which is typically at least 4-byte aligned for ints.\n\nWe'll also change the loops to use pointer arithmetic? Not necessary.\n\nI'll propose this transformation with hypothesis: \"Flattening the matrix into a contiguous 1D array improves cache locality and enables auto-vectorization of row-update loops, while preserving correctness. Adding alignment hints further aids SIMD.\"\n\nTransformations: [\"flatten matrix to 1D vector\", \"add __builtin_assume_aligned for data pointer\"].\n\nWe must ensure we don't break correctness. The snake traversal logic remains the same; just index calculations change.\n\nLet's write the new code:\n\n- Replace std::vector<std::vector<int>> with std::vector<int> mat(n*n);\n- Read input: for i in 0..n-1, for j in 0..n-1, scanf(\"%d\", &mat[i*n + j]);\n- In loops:\n   - Initial sum: for i=0;i<s;i++) for(j=0;j<s;j++) sum += mat[i*n + j];\n   - Row update: exitingRow = i; enteringRow = i+s;\n        for(k=0;k<s;k++) { sum -= mat[exitingRow*n + (j+k)]; }\n        for(k=0;k<s;k++) { sum += mat[enteringRow*n + (j+k)]; }\n   - Column update: exitingCol, enteringCol as before.\n        for(k=0;k<s;k++) {sum -= mat[(i+k)*n + exitingCol]; }\n        for(k=0;k<s;k++) {sum += mat[(i+k)*n + enteringCol]; }\n\nWe also need to ensure that the vector is not reallocated; we can reserve size n*n and then use push_back? Better: allocate with constructor std::vector<int> mat(n*n);